{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothAlgebraicInteger","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothAlgebraicInteger","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothTaylorBound","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.NumberField.House","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.NumberField.Norm","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Norm.Transitivity"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.norm_embedding_coeff_translate_le_index","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.norm_embedding_coeff_translate_le_index","kind":"theorem","line":271,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L271-L314","doc":"**The Taylor bound at the distinguished embedding.**  This is the composition that makes the\nassembly honest: `hidx` is step 1's index at `α`, and it is what restricts the sum, so the estimate\ngenuinely consumes step 1 instead of taking its consequence for granted.\n\nAn assembly that instead took the smallness as a hypothesis would type-check and would never use\nstep 1 at all — the polynomial's defining property would be inert. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.norm_embedding_coeff_translate_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.norm_embedding_coeff_translate_le","kind":"theorem","line":253,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L253-L269","doc":"**The trivial bound, at any embedding.**  No smallness is claimed — just the height budget with\nthe translation point's own size.  This is what each of the `d−1` other conjugates contributes to\n`eq_zero_of_norm_mul_pow_lt_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.embedding_coeff_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.embedding_coeff_translate","kind":"theorem","line":235,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L235-L251","doc":"**An embedding commutes with translation at a rational point.**  `φ` fixes `ℚ`, so the point\ndoes not move under `φ`; only the coefficients do.  That is why the same rational `β` can be used\nat every conjugate, and why the `d−1` \"other\" bounds are about the same polynomial evaluated the\nsame way. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.submultSeminorm_norm_complex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.submultSeminorm_norm_complex","kind":"theorem","line":220,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L220-L227","doc":"**`‖·‖` on `ℂ` is a submultiplicative seminorm.**  So every estimate in `RothTaylorBound`\napplies at a complex embedding of a number field — which is exactly what the `𝓞 K` case needs,\nsince there the smallness is measured at one embedding while the others carry only the `house`\nbound.  Generalising those estimates from `|·|` on `ℝ` to an arbitrary `SubmultSeminorm` was\ncheaper than a second copy of the peeling induction, and this is the whole cost of the\n`ℂ` case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_of_norm_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_of_norm_lt","kind":"theorem","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L177-L218","doc":"**Step 2's index lower bound over `𝓞 K`.**  Every low-weight translated coefficient that is\nsmall at `φ₀` and bounded at the other conjugates vanishes, and enough vanishing is a lower bound\non the index.\n\nThe hypothesis is a *pair* per coefficient, unlike the `ℤ` case's single bound: `M` at the other\n`d−1` conjugates and smallness at `φ₀` measured against `M^{d−1}`.  That asymmetry is the whole\ndifference between `|N(x)| ≥ 1` and `|z| ≥ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_ringOfIntegers_mul_coeff_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_ringOfIntegers_mul_coeff_translate","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L145-L175","doc":"**`∏ q_h^{r_h}` clears the denominator over `𝓞 K` too.**  The rational-point matrix entries are\nrational, so the `ℤ` computation is reused verbatim; what changes is only that the coefficients it\nis multiplied against are algebraic integers rather than rational ones. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_int_mul_translateMatrix_deg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_int_mul_translateMatrix_deg","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L122-L141","doc":"The per-monomial denominator statement with the degree bound folded in. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_preimage_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_preimage_sum","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L109-L120","doc":"A finite sum of elements each in the image of a ring hom is in the image. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.norm_le_house","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.norm_le_house","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L97-L104","doc":"**`house` bounds every conjugate.**  The bridge from step 1's height estimate, which is stated\nwith `house`, to the per-embedding bounds `eq_zero_of_norm_mul_pow_lt_one` consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.eq_zero_of_norm_mul_pow_lt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.eq_zero_of_norm_mul_pow_lt_one","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L70-L95","doc":"**Small at one place, bounded at the others, forces zero.**  The `𝓞 K` analogue of\n`eq_zero_of_abs_lt_of_den`, and note the extra input: `M` must bound *every other* conjugate.  Over\n`ℤ` there are no other conjugates and the hypothesis is vacuous, which is why the `ℤ` statement\nlooks simpler than it is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.one_le_prod_norm_embeddings","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.one_le_prod_norm_embeddings","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L59-L68","doc":"**The replacement for `1 ≤ |z|`.**  A non-zero algebraic integer has `∏_φ ‖φ x‖ ≥ 1` — the\nproduct, not any single factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.prod_norm_embeddings_eq_abs_norm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.prod_norm_embeddings_eq_abs_norm","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothAlgebraicInteger.lean#L45-L57","doc":"**The product formula over embeddings.**  `∏_φ ‖φ x‖ = |N_{K/ℚ}(x)|`, indexed by ring\nhomomorphisms `K →+* ℂ` — the indexing `house` uses. ","signatureChunk":0}]}